    "updated_at": "2025-12-29T19:51:23Z",
    "body": "Im no coder (except hello world in python) but I used cursor to troubleshoot this issue. After I posted the bug report I pulled the whole repo and asked Opus 4.5 to do a thorough investigation of the codebase and try to identify the cause. This is the response. I have no ability my self to check this, so take it for what it is, if it makes sense and are of help, that's good. If its not, ignore it:\n\n**Code-level findings from investigation:**\n\nAfter analyzing the Homarr codebase, these are the likely causes:\n\n1. **WebSocket created at module load time** (`apps/nextjs/src/app/[locale]/_client-providers/trpc.tsx:47-49`):\n   ```javascript\n   const wsClient = createWSClient({ url: constructWebsocketUrl() });\n   ```\n   The WebSocket is created immediately when the module loads, not lazily.\n\n2. **httpBatchStreamLink for tRPC** (`trpc.tsx:93-98`):\n   Uses HTTP streaming (JSONL) which keeps HTTP/2 streams open during batched responses.\n\n3. **25+ active WebSocket subscriptions** across widgets:\n   - `updatedPing`, `subscribeAtLocation`, `subscribeToEvents`, `subscribeToSummary`, `subscribeContainers`, etc.\n   - Each widget opens a subscription stream over the single WebSocket.\n\n4. **ReactQueryStreamedHydration** (`trpc.tsx:108`):\n   Enables streaming hydration which keeps HTTP responses open for progressive loading.\n\n5. **39+ useSuspenseQuery calls**:\n   These may batch requests that keep streams open while waiting.\n\n**Hypothesis:** The combination of multiple WebSocket subscriptions + streaming HTTP responses + batched queries may exhaust available HTTP/2 streams on the shared connection, blocking new connections like TrueNAS's WebSocket.\n\n**Suggested fixes:**\n1. Create WebSocket lazily (only when subscriptions are needed)\n2. Close WebSocket on page visibility change (when tab is hidden)\n3. Consider switching from `httpBatchStreamLink` to `httpBatchLink` (non-streaming)\n4. Limit concurrent active subscriptions\n5. Add cleanup logic when navigating away",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
